\pard\sl240\slmult1 The 6-pin serial port follows the FTDI standard and should be a male 0.1" header to match the female connector \par
used on most USB-to-Serial boards. \par
The LED was moved to PA3 to free up PA7 since it is the recieve in (RxD) pin. PA6 is the transmit out (TxD) pin.\par
(see Table 5.2 USART0)[2]\par
NOTE: Figure III-1 shows the BOARD pins numbers, NOT the ATtiny412 device pin numbers.\par
\par
Optionally, an Arduino board can be used as something to talk to. Connect RxD to Arduino TxD and TxD to \par
Arduino RxD. Be sure to use the Arduino +5v power for the ATtiny412 instead of the battery pack.\par
\par
Section 24[2] describes the USART operation and its registers. Two files are added to the project:\par
\tab Header Files > SerialPoll.h\par
\tab Source Files > serialPoll.c\par
These will contain the serial support code. A simple polling process will be used. See [ref] for ideas on how \par
to impliment an interupt based system.\par
The USART will be set up for Full-Duplex, Asynchronous, 8N1 communications at 9600 baud. Section 24.3.1[2] \par
lists the steps needed to initialize the USART for this mode.\par
1. Baud rate: USART0.BAUD sets the BAUD rate (i.e. the bit rate of communications). The following lines of \par
code will calculate the baud rate based on the CPU clock rate.\par
\f1\fs20   #define F_CPU 20000000L\tab\tab // Clock Speed 20 MHz\par
  #define USART_BAUD_RATE(BAUD_RATE) ((float)(F_CPU * 64)/(16 * (float)BAUD_RATE)+0.5)\fs22\par
\f0 and \par
\f1\fs20   USART0_BAUD = (uint16_t)USART_BAUD_RATE(baud);\f0\fs22\par
will set the baud rate.\par
2. Frame format: USART0.CTRLC sets the frame format. The register is set to Asychronous 8N1 with predifined \par
bit group code (_gc) found in the iotn412h file.\par
\f1\fs20   USART0_CTRLC = USART_CHSIZE_8BIT_gc;\f0\fs22\par
3. TxD as an output pin: Set the DIR register to make pin PA6 an output. PA7 (RxD will be an input by default.\par
\f1\fs20   PORTA_DIR |= PIN6_bm;\tab\tab // TxD as an OUTPUT\f0\fs22\par
4. Enable transmitter and receiver: Use CTRLB to enable the reciever and transmitter.\par
\f1\fs20   USART0_CTRLB |= (USART_RXEN_bm | USART_TXEN_bm);\f0\fs22\par
All of this can be gathered into an initialization subroutine as shown below.\par
\f1\fs20   /* Initialized USART0 registers and IO pins */\par
  void USART0_init(uint16_t baud)\par
  \{\par
    /* Set IO port pin directions. */\par
    PORTA_DIR &= ~PIN7_bm;\tab\tab // Rx as INPUT (default)\par
    PORTA_DIR |= PIN6_bm;\tab\tab // Tx as OUTPUT\par
\tab\par
    /* Set baud rate */\par
    USART0_BAUD = (uint16_t)USART_BAUD_RATE(baud);\par
\par
    /* Set frame format: 8bit Data, No Parity, 1stop bit (8N1) */\par
    USART0_CTRLC = USART_CHSIZE_8BIT_gc;\tab // default\par
\par
    /* Enable receiver and transmitter */\par
    USART0_CTRLB |= (USART_RXEN_bm | USART_TXEN_bm);\par
  \}\f0\fs22\par
\par
If a byte is written into the TXDATAL register, the USART will transmit it out the TxD line. The register should \par
be checked to see if it's empty before writting to it to prevent an overwrite. The example code below does this.\par
\f1\fs20   /* Blocking call to send one character. */\par
  void USART0_sendChar(char data)\par
  \{\par
    /* Wait for empty transmit buffer */\par
    while ( !(USART0_STATUS & USART_DREIF_bm ))\tab ;\par
\par
    /* Put data into buffer, sends the data */\par
    USART0_TXDATAL = data;\par
  \}\par
\f0\fs22 This is a 'blocking call' because the function does not immediatly return and if the USART failed to send the last byte, it will wait forever. A way to midigate this is to add a timeout to the wait operation.\par
\par
To receive a byte, just check the USART_RXCIF bit in the STATUS register and read the RXDATAL register if the \par
bit is set. The example code below shows one way to do this.\par
\f1\fs20   /* Blocking call to receive one character.\par
   * USART0_isChar() should be called and return 'true' before calling this function.\par
   */\par
  char USART0_recvChar()\par
  \{\par
    /* Wait for data to be received */\par
    while ( !(USART0_STATUS & USART_RXCIF_bm) )\tab ;\par
\tab\par
    /* Get and return received data from buffer */\par
    return USART0_RXDATAL;\par
  \}\par
\par
  // Check for character in Receive register\par
  bool USART0_isChar()\par
  \{\par
    bool result = false;\par
\tab\par
    if( USART0_STATUS & USART_RXCIF_bm ) \{\par
      result = true;\par
    \}\par
\tab\par
    return( result );\par
  \}\f0\fs22\par
